Considering the block criteria: "If the ticket in question involves a requirements change / destructive operation / high-risk decision, output BLOCKED on the final line." The plan for this ticket stays within the permitted scope (scripts/save/**, schema, examples, tests). The migration policy rejects unsafe v1 saves — the ticket brief itself explicitly demands that ("Must propose a v1→new-version migration with no silent loss; clearly state the safe handling when v1 has no fingerprint ledger for processed transactions; fabricating idempotent data is prohibited") — so this isn't a unilateral requirements change, it's a required deliverable. Interface gaps (G-1..G-4) are flagged for review rather than unilaterally fixed — that's exactly what the brief demands ("First point out interface gaps and decide in independent review. Do not cross boundaries on your own"). Phase B scope fits entirely within permitted files (new scripts/save files with new class name AtomicSaveService to avoid collision, schema v2 rewrite, tests). No economic parameters, no UI, no product scope changes. The delegation-based approval (Codex's per-ticket review) is already on record in REQUIREMENTS_CHANGELOG. So this is not a blocked case — proceed with PHASE-A-DONE.
